## Start with `profiling_capabilities`

Call `profiling_capabilities()` first, every round. It reports what this
host actually supports: rocprofv3/ROCm version, whether the ATT trace
decoder is installed, the PMC counter-set catalogue for the detected
architecture, and whether `rocprof-compute`'s Python dependency set is
importable. Use it to rule out an altitude up front (e.g. no ATT decoder
means go straight to `profile_counters`/`profile_kernel_deep` for
hardware-ceiling evidence instead), not to discover this by a failed
capture.

## Altitudes: which tool answers which question

Start at the system timeline unless you already have strong evidence the
bottleneck is kernel-local or library-selection. Descend only as far as the
evidence demands; each altitude below costs more overhead than the last.

| Altitude | Answers | Capture tool | Drill-downs |
| -------- | ------- | ------------- | ----------- |
| System timeline | host/device overlap, idle gaps, launch cost, HIP graph coverage, library selection (AITER/CK/hipBLASLt/Triton/torch-native) | `profile_timeline` | `kernels`, `families`, `idle_gaps`, `cpu_overhead`, `memory`, `graphs`, `host_idle`, `query`, `summary`, `captures` |
| Counters (PMC) | which hardware ceiling one specific kernel is against | `profile_counters` | `counter_report`, `counter_triage` |
| Kernel-internal | full Speed-of-Light + roofline for one targeted kernel | `profile_kernel_deep` | `compute_analyze` |
| Instruction-level | per-instruction stalls inside one kernel, one compute unit (version-gated, see Pitfalls) | `profile_instructions` | `att_hotspots` |
| In-process torch capture | per-op/kernel torch.profiler trace, optionally windowed on an already-running process | `profile_ops` (delegates to the torch plugin) | `certify`, `gemm_shapes`, `roofline`, `summary` |
| Paired A/B | did a change actually move the needle, same session, drift-controlled | (compare two captures) | `compare`, `bench_verdict` |

Every `profile_*` capture tool shares the same lifecycle arguments
(`command`, `env`, `cwd`, `ready_command`, `load_command`, `stop_signal`,
`grace_s`, `timeout_s`), plus arguments scoped to what it captures (counter
sets, target kernel regex, target CU, ...). **What goes into those
arguments is engine-specific, not a ROCm concern**: see
`serving-systems/references/tooling/profiling-serving-engines.md` for the
index of what each argument is for, and the linked per-engine file (under
`serving-systems/references/engines/`) for the engine actually in play:
single-process launch settings, graceful-shutdown behavior, and any
torch.profiler contract differences. This template covers only the
ROCm-specific mechanics below.

## `{{ profiler_mcp_name }}` MCP tools OR shell

If the `{{ profiler_mcp_name }}` MCP server is attached to this round, call its tools directly (argument names shown). Without it, run the equivalent `rocprofv3`/`rocprof-compute` command yourself (see `serving-systems/references/platforms/rocm/profiler.md` for exact flags) and shell out to the matching drill-down subcommand on `python {{ profiler_support_name }}/<module>.py` against its output.

### System trace: `profile_timeline` + drill-downs (`analyze_rocprof.py`)

`profile_timeline` runs an `rocprofv3` system-trace capture end to end
(launch, wait for `ready_command` if given, run `load_command` if given,
stop with `stop_signal` after `grace_s`, wait out the post-exit flush) and
returns a capture id.

**A server capture defaults to the load-phase window, not the whole run.**
A capture that wraps a server under load also captures that server's own
startup ahead of it (weight load, warmup, KV init, ...), which can dwarf the
actual benchmarked traffic and pollute every table below with one-time
setup work. Every drill-down in the table accepts a `window` argument
(`'load'` by default when the capture recorded one, `'all'`, `'startup'`, or
an explicit `'start_s:end_s'`) and its output header states which window it
used and how many of the total dispatches that covers. Pass `window='all'`
explicitly to see the whole run, or `window='startup'` to look at the
excluded setup phase itself.

| Drill-down | Purpose |
| ---------- | ------- |
| `kernels(capture, top=15)` | Top GPU kernels by total time, with a library-family column. |
| `families(capture)` | GPU time grouped by kernel library family (AITER/CK/hipBLASLt/Triton/torch-native). A fallback share where a fused-library share was expected means a silent fallback, not a hardware limit. |
| `idle_gaps(capture, top=10)` | Largest GPU idle gaps between kernel launches. |
| `cpu_overhead(capture)` | HIP API launch overhead and launch-bound heuristic. |
| `memory(capture)` | Memory copies by direction, bytes, bandwidth. |
| `graphs(capture)` | HIP graph launches and attribution-degradation check. |
| `host_idle(capture)` | Validity check: detects a mostly host-idle / load-missed capture. |
| `query(capture, sql)` | Arbitrary SQL, but only against a rocpd SQLite export (ROCm 7+'s `--output-format rocpd`); not usable against the CSV/JSON output every other drill-down here reads. |
| `summary(capture, top=15)` | All-in-one: kernels + families + idle_gaps + cpu_overhead + memory + graphs. |
| `captures()` | List captures already taken this session. |

**Validity first.** Before trusting any bottleneck read, check `host_idle` (a
mostly-idle capture usually means the wrong process was captured: the API
server/driver, not the worker issuing HIP kernels) and `graphs` (confirms
whether decode is running under a captured HIP graph or eager; the two need
different follow-up: replay timing and inter-replay gaps for graphed decode,
`idle_gaps`/`cpu_overhead` for eager).

### PMC counters: `profile_counters` + drill-downs (`counters.py`): one hardware ceiling for one hot kernel

`profile_counters` packs the requested counter sets into as few rocprofv3
hardware passes as it can (real MI210 validation: `mfma`+`hbm` and
`mfma`+`l2` pack into one pass; `l2`+`hbm` do not and stay separate) instead
of always spending one pass per set, then automatically falls back to
splitting a pass rocprofv3 rejects. **Each pass, not each requested set,
re-runs the whole profiled workload from scratch**: the tool's output
states how many passes were planned vs. actually run (a fallback split
raises the run count above the plan), so read that before assuming
`len(sets)` full workload runs. Target one already-identified hot kernel;
this is not a whole-run tool.

| Drill-down | Purpose |
| ---------- | ------- |
| `counter_report(dirs, kernel=None, top=15, arch=None)` | Merge PMC passes per kernel; print resource usage + derived metrics (L2 hit rate, HBM bandwidth, MFMA issue rate, LDS bank-conflict rate). |
| `counter_triage(dirs, arch, kernel=None, top=15)` | Classify each hot kernel's bottleneck (occupancy/bandwidth/compute/LDS/launch/latency) with evidence and a next lever. |

### Kernel-internal: `profile_kernel_deep` + drill-down (`compute.py`, rocprof-compute)

`profile_kernel_deep` runs `rocprof-compute profile` against one targeted
kernel and returns a capture id. Check `profiling_capabilities` first:
`rocprof-compute` refuses to run at all unless its Python dependency set is
importable in whichever interpreter runs it, an all-or-nothing preflight
better hit before a capture than during one.

**The target-kernel argument matches a literal substring, not a friendly
name: verify it first.** Confirmed on a real capture: torch GEMMs dispatch
through rocBLAS/hipBLASLt as Tensile kernels named like
`Cijk_Ailk_Bljk_BBS_BH_..._MT256x128x32_...`, which does **not** contain the
substring `"gemm"`: a naive `"gemm"` filter matches zero dispatches. List
real kernel names with `profile_timeline` + `kernels` first, rather than
guessing and hitting rocprof-compute's own confusing `KeyError: 'Grid_Size'`
crash several steps later.

| Drill-down | Purpose |
| ---------- | ------- |
| `compute_analyze(workload_dir, blocks=..., max_stat=10, kernel="", timeout=300)` | Run `rocprof-compute analyze`; falls back to a summarized read of the raw CSVs (top kernels by time, a couple of rough counter ratios) if it fails. |

### Instruction-level: `profile_instructions` + drill-down (`att.py`, Advanced Thread Trace): version-gated

`profile_instructions` runs an `rocprofv3 --att` capture and decode against
one targeted kernel and returns a decoded dispatch directory (or a clear
error if the decoder isn't available). ATT needs `rocprofv3` >= ROCm 7.1
(the `--att`/thread-trace flag doesn't exist before that) and the separate
`rocprof-trace-decoder` shared library, which ships as its own GitHub
release, not inside any ROCm module; `profiling_capabilities` reports
whether it's installed and where. A missing-decoder error means it isn't
installed or isn't pointed at yet: the library is a single small `.so`
extractable from the release tarball with no build step and no root, so try
installing it into a scratch directory before giving up on ATT for the
session; if that isn't practical in the time available, treat the error as
"ATT unavailable on this host," not a usage bug, and fall back to
`profile_counters`/`profile_kernel_deep` for hardware-ceiling evidence
instead. Only reach for ATT after counters already point at a specific
stall class; it attributes per-instruction stalls inside one kernel, one
compute unit, and costs the most overhead of any tool here.

| Drill-down | Purpose |
| ---------- | ------- |
| `att_hotspots(dispatch_dir, top=15)` | Top stall hotspots (instructions + source lines) from a decoded ATT dispatch directory. |

### Warm targets: repeated windows without relaunching a `rocprofv3` capture

Every `profile_*` capture tool above launches its own target for the whole
capture's lifetime; none of them accept `target=` (a real capability probe
`profiling_capabilities` runs confirms whether the host's
`librocprofiler-register` build even supports `rocprofv3 --attach`, but
VibeSys does not implement attach-based capture regardless of what that
probe reports -- passing `target=` returns a clear error naming this). For
repeated windows against the same already-running process, use the torch
plugin's `start_target(command, ...)` + `profile_ops(target=<id>,
load_command=..., duration_s=...)` instead (delegated here, same as a
direct `profile_ops` call): this launches once, keeps the process warm
between windows, and costs one fresh launch total instead of one per
window. `stop_target(target)` when done, or let the server's own exit stop
it. This only gets an in-process torch.profiler trace, not a system-wide
rocprofv3 trace or PMC counters -- use it to compare op-level shapes
before/after on the same warmed-up process, not as a substitute for
`profile_timeline`/`profile_counters`.

### Paired A/B: `compare` / `bench_verdict`: did the change actually help

This verifies; it does not diagnose. Use it to confirm a hypothesis already
localized by the tools above, same session, drift-controlled (alternate
reference/candidate reps, never compare across sessions or boxes).

| Tool | Purpose |
| ---- | ------- |
| `compare(a, b, threshold_pct=3.0)` | Unpaired median comparison of two recorded (non-interleaved) sample sets. |
| `bench_verdict(samples, threshold_pct=3.0)` | Paired A/B verdict from a file of genuinely interleaved recorded pairs, the preferred shape for "did this help." |

## Capturing a profile

Capture is a long-running step; call the appropriate `profile_*` tool
before calling any drill-down. **A server capture can take 10-25 minutes**
(weight load, warmup, the benchmark run, then the post-stop flush): if your
MCP client enforces its own tool-call timeout, it must be set above the
capture's `timeout_s`, not left at whatever default applies to a normal,
fast tool call. Abandoning a call (a client-side timeout, or a disconnect)
does stop the capture server-side -- the target process tree is torn down
the same way a `timeout_s` expiry would be -- but you lose the response
text, and calling a `profile_*` tool again immediately afterward can get a
"busy: ..." reply while that teardown is still settling: this server runs
one GPU-using capture at a time per process. `captures()` returns
immediately (even while a capture is in flight) and can confirm what
actually happened to a call whose response you lost.

1. Read the objective, candidate contract, manifest, build/startup commands, and benchmark `--help` output to understand which implementation and process the workload exercises{% if benchmark_command is defined and benchmark_command %}: `{{ benchmark_command }} --help`{% endif %}. Do not assume a filename, language, or launcher.
2. Stop any stale candidate processes from previous attempts.
3. Fill `command`/`env`/`ready_command`/`load_command`/`setup_command`/`stop_signal`/`grace_s` from the candidate's declared startup contract and, where the candidate is a known serving engine, that engine's file under `serving-systems/references/engines/` (start from the index at `serving-systems/references/tooling/profiling-serving-engines.md`). `command`/`ready_command`/`load_command`/`setup_command` may be multi-line shell text (heredocs, embedded quotes, a multi-line `python3 -c "..."`): each is written to its own script file before being run, so nothing between here and the shell that executes it re-tokenizes it. **Avoid `$(...)` command substitution for a value the launch command needs (e.g. picking a free port)** inside `command`/`ready_command`/`load_command`: the profiling tool is injected into every process in that launched tree, including a small helper spawned just for that substitution, and a diagnostic line that helper's injected tool prints to its own stdout lands inside the captured value and corrupts it (seen for real: a port helper's `$(...)` output picked up an unrelated diagnostic line and the resulting `--port <corrupted>` argument made the real target reject its own arguments; confirmed on real MI210 hardware that no profiler-side env var or flag suppresses this). The safe pattern: compute the value in `setup_command` -- it runs to completion *before* `command`, entirely outside the profiler -- with a plain redirect, then have `command` read it back with the `read` builtin: `setup_command` does `python3 -c "..." > /tmp/port`, `command` does `read -r PORT < /tmp/port`, never `$(cat /tmp/port)` or any other `$(...)` form, which reintroduces the same injection risk one step later since `cat` is itself a forked process running inside `command`'s tree. A nonzero `setup_command` exit stops the capture immediately (no target ever started) rather than letting a corrupted value reach the target. **Prefer a single-process, non-serving `command`** that exits on its own once the workload finishes over wrapping a long-running server: `rocprofv3` only flushes its trace buffers on a clean process exit, so a server that gets killed instead of exiting cleanly can silently lose the trace even though the run otherwise looked fine. If a server capture is unavoidable, set `stop_signal` to a graceful signal (never a hard kill of the process tree) and give `grace_s` a generous value: the post-exit flush to disk can itself take minutes and scales with how much was traced, not with how long the run took.
4. Pre-warm under load before the traced window starts (CRITICAL on ROCm: first-time AITER/Triton JIT resolution and any `TunableOp`/AITER online tuning take minutes and must not land inside the captured window). This is also where HIP-graph capture happens for graph-mode serving; keep it outside the traced window so the trace holds only steady-state replay kernels, not one-time capture kernels. Use `load_command` for a separate untraced warmup phase if the tool supports one, or bake a warmup step into `command` itself.
5. Identify the process that actually issues HIP kernels before scoping the capture. A serving engine's request-handling worker commonly runs in a process separate from its API server/driver; a driver-only capture shows near-zero GPU activity by construction: that is "wrong process," not "the GPU is idle." For tensor-parallel serving, capture each worker independently.
6. Keep the traced window representative and request CSV-only output where the tool exposes that choice: a multi-minute full trace is unreadable, and JSON output duplicates every CSV's content inside one blob and can reach GB scale at serving-workload row counts for zero extra information. Request the extra HIP-runtime-trace kind only when `cpu_overhead`/`graphs`/launch-bound analysis is actually needed: it traces one HIP runtime API call per kernel launch/memcpy/event, so on a batched-decode workload it typically runs 2-4x the size of the kernel trace it's paired with.
7. Wait for the `profile_*` call to return a capture id before treating capture as done; it already waits out the post-exit flush. Stop any other background candidate process you started.
8. Call `summary(capture)` or a specific drill-down against the returned capture id.

**Eager vs. HIP-graph attribution.** Check `graphs` before reading per-op
timing as ground truth: a small number of large graph-launch entries covering
decode means decode runs under a captured HIP graph, and per-op eager numbers
elsewhere in the trace do not attribute to it: use replay timing and
inter-replay scheduling gaps instead. One entry per op instead of graph-launch
entries means decode is eager; `idle_gaps` and `cpu_overhead` are the right
tools for launch-bound vs. kernel-bound there. Do not sum per-op eager timings
and call the result graph-era throughput.

